---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="How it works">
  <h1>How it works</h1>
  <p>
    The website sends static application files to your browser. Your message is
    encrypted in that browser; no encryption or decryption server processes it.
    Confirm the recipient's full public-key fingerprint through your existing
    trusted contact before encrypting.
  </p>
  <figure aria-labelledby="flow-caption">
    <figcaption id="flow-caption">Local sender to offline recipient</figcaption>
    <ol class="flow">
      <li>
        <strong>Your browser</strong>
        <span>
          Message + confirmed public key → native WebCrypto encryption
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your explicit export</strong>
        <span>Copy raw or download ciphertext.txt; artwork is optional</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Recipient's offline Go tool</strong>
        <span>
          Encrypted private key + hidden local passphrase → authenticate → new
          private plaintext file
        </span>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </figure>
  <h2>Encryption and transport</h2>
  <p>
    Each message uses a fresh 32-byte AES key and 12-byte nonce. RSA-OAEP with
    SHA-256 wraps that key using the selected RSA-3072 or RSA-4096 public key.
    AES-256-GCM encrypts exact UTF-8 bytes with a 128-bit authentication tag;
    the wrapped key and nonce are associated data. The compatible envelope is
    wrapped key, nonce, tag, ciphertext, encoded as unpadded canonical
    Base64URL.
  </p>
  <p>
    Send the complete raw ciphertext.txt to the recipient. The Go receiver
    unlocks the encrypted private PEM locally, unwraps the AES key and checks
    authentication before creating a new plaintext file. It never listens for
    connections, prints plaintext, or uploads private material. Ordinary use
    needs one command; <a href="/receive/">see the receiving guide</a>.
  </p>
  <h2>Glyphs and checks</h2>
  <p>
    The mixed display shows ciphertext characters and outlined symbols, with
    three null shapes that carry no payload. This is reversible presentation,
    not extra security. PNG and SVG artwork are optional; the v1 receiver reads
    raw text and cannot decrypt artwork. Printed raw rows and public check codes
    help locate transcription mistakes; they do not authenticate a sender.
  </p>
  <p>
    Send raw ciphertext with the artwork. Only the matching private key can
    normally decrypt it. Public-key encryption does not identify the sender.
  </p>
  <p>
    Encryption does not hide message length, provide forward secrecy or resist a
    future quantum attack on RSA. Integrated independent review and signed
    release verification remain pending.
  </p>
</Layout>

<style>
  figure {
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }
  figcaption {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .flow {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    padding-inline-start: 1.5rem;
    max-width: 70ch;
  }
  .flow li {
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
    background: var(--color-panel);
  }
  .flow span {
    display: block;
  }
</style>
